The `diff` command has a built‑in option that prints the name of the C function that each change belongs to.

```bash
diff -p   # or  diff --show-c-function
```

### What it does
- `-p` / `--show-c-function`: After each hunk of changes, `diff` prints a line like  

  ```
  Function: my_function
  ```

  indicating the most recent C function definition that the surrounding lines belong to.

### Example

Suppose you have two versions of a file `example.c`:

```c
/* example.c */
#include <stdio.h>

int add(int a, int b) {
    return a + b;
}

int main(void) {
    printf("%d\n", add(1, 2));
    return 0;
}
```

Running:

```bash
diff -p old.c new.c
```

might produce output such as:

```
--- old.c   2025-01-15 10:00:00.000000000 +0000
+++ new.c   2025-01-15 10:05:00.000000000 +0000
@@ -1,7 +1,7 @@
 int add(int a, int b) {
     return a + b;
 }
+int subtract(int a, int b) {
+    return a - b;
+}
 int main(void) {
     printf("%d\n", add(1, 2));
     return 0;
 }
```

The line `int add(int a, int b) {` appears as the “C function” header, showing that the surrounding changes are inside `add`.

### Other useful options (if you need more control)

| Option | Effect |
|--------|--------|
| `-u` / `--unified` | Produce a unified diff (default style) |
| `-c` / `-C NUM` | Show `NUM` lines of context (default 3) |
| `-y` / `--side-by-side` | Show both files side‑by‑side |
| `-w` / `--ignore-all-space` | Ignore whitespace differences (useful for function signatures) |
| `-i` / `--ignore-case` | Ignore case differences |

Combine them as needed, e.g.:

```bash
diff -p -u old.c new.c
```

This will give you a unified diff with the C‑function headers.

